content.text:
I fixed the bug: scoped styles now give `@media` blocks inside nested CSS the same `&[data-v-…]` wrapper as the parent rule, so the media query's styles no longer lose to the base ones.

**The cause:** when a scoped rule contains a nested rule (like the empty `.bar {}`), the compiler moves the rule's own declarations into a `&[data-v-…]` block. It didn't do the same for at-rules such as `@media` or `@container` inside that rule, so their declarations had no scope attribute and less specificity than the wrapped base styles.

**The fix** is in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`. A new helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`, does the wrapping for the rule itself and for each at-rule directly inside it. It now moves comments into the wrapper along with declarations. Inside nested rules marked `:deep`, nothing is wrapped, as before.

I tested it with a temporary spec (since deleted) on this input:
`.foo { color: red; .bar {} @media (max-width: 800px) { color: green; .baz {} } }`

which now compiles to:
```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { color: red }
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { color: green }
.baz[data-v-test] {}
}
}
```
All 25 existing tests in `compileStyle.spec.ts` still pass. I didn't add a permanent test for this case.

